{title: It Came Upon the Midnight Clear}
{artist: Edmund Sears & Richard Willis}
{key: Eb}
{time: 6/8}
{tempo: 80}

Verse 1
It [Eb]came upon the [Ab]midnight clear, that [C]glorious song of [Bb]old,
from [Eb]angels bending [Ab]near the earth to [Bb]touch their harps of [Eb]gold:
"Peace on the earth, good [Ab]will to men, from [C]heaven's all-gracious [Bb]King" —
the [Eb]world in solemn [Ab]stillness lay to [Bb]hear the angels [Eb]sing.

Verse 2
Still [Eb]through the cloven [Ab]skies they [C]come With peaceful [Bb]wings
unfurled, [Eb]And still [Ab]their heavenly music [Bb]floats O'er all the [Eb]weary
world; Above its sad and [Ab]lowly plains, They [C]bend on [Bb]hov'ring wing,
And [Eb]ever over its [Ab]Babel sounds [Bb]The blessèd angels [Eb]sing.

Verse 3
Yet [Eb]with the woes [Ab]of sin and strife [C]The world has [Bb]suffered
long; [Eb]Beneath the [Ab]angel strain have rolled Two [Bb]thousand years of wrong; [Eb]And
man, at war with man, [Ab]hears not The love- song [C]which they [Bb]bring; O
hush [Eb]the noise, ye [Ab]men of strife And [Bb]hear the angels [Eb]sing.

Verse 4
And [Eb]ye, beneath life's [Ab]crushing load, Whose [C]forms are bending [Bb]low,
Who [Eb]toil along [Ab]the climbing way [Bb]With painful steps and [Eb]slow,
Look now! for glad and [Ab]golden hours Come [C]swiftly on [Bb]the wing.
O [Eb]rest beside the [Ab]weary road, And [Bb]hear the angels [Eb]sing!

Verse 5
For [Eb]lo! the days [Ab]are hast'ning on, [C]By prophet- bards [Bb]foretold,
When [Eb]with the [Ab]ever circling years Comes [Bb]round the age of [Eb]gold;
When peace shall over all [Ab]the earth Its ancient [C]splendors fling, [Bb]And the
whole [Eb]world send back [Ab]the song Which [Bb]now the angels [Eb]sing.
